      "task_id": "h001",
      "question": "CR-2311 rotated the mesh certificate authority. What does the step that decides whether a rotation is safe to start actually compare?",
      "category": "multi-hop",
      "expected": "answer",
      "attempts": [
        {
          "task_id": "h001",
          "trial": 1,
          "text": "The step that decides whether a rotation is safe to start compares the shortest remaining leaf certificate lifetime across every counted service against the rotation window. If the shortest remaining lifetime is less than the rotation window, the rotation should not start because it would cause leaves to expire before they can be re-issued. This comparison ensures the rotation timing is safe to avoid service disruption. This is described in CR-2311 and the referenced procedure RB-207, specifically in the step that compares the shortest remaining leaf lifetime against the rotation window [1][5].",
          "citations": [
            {
              "marker": 1,
              "document_id": "change-2311-certificate-rotation",
              "chunk_id": "change-2311-certificate-rotation:2",
              "source_uri": "/home/sergi/projects/paimon/evaluation/corpus/heldout/change-2311-certificate-rotation.md",
              "title": "Change record CR-2311: rotate the mesh certificate authority",
              "heading_path": [
                "Change record CR-2311: rotate the mesh certificate authority",
                "How it was done"
              ],
              "start_char": 380,
              "end_char": 743,
              "quote": "## How it was done\n\nThe operator followed **RB-207** end to end and did not deviate. The step that\ndecides whether a rotation is safe to start is the one that compares the\nshortest remaining leaf lifetime against the rotation window; this change record\ndoes not restate it, because a procedure copied into a change record is a\nprocedure that stops being updated.\n"
            },
            {
              "marker": 5,
              "document_id": "runbook-rb-207-certificate-rotation",
              "chunk_id": "runbook-rb-207-certificate-rotation:2",
              "source_uri": "/home/sergi/projects/paimon/evaluation/corpus/heldout/runbook-rb-207-certificate-rotation.md",
              "title": "RB-207: rotating the mesh certificate authority",
              "heading_path": [
                "RB-207: rotating the mesh certificate authority",
                "Steps"
              ],
              "start_char": 376,
              "end_char": 1108,
              "quote": "## Steps\n\n1. Take the service count from the mesh control plane. Do not use the inventory\n   label: a service missing its label is a service that will not be counted and\n   will not be rotated.\n\n2. Read the shortest remaining leaf lifetime across every counted service.\n\n3. **Compare that lifetime against the rotation window.** If the shortest\n   remaining lifetime is less than the window, stop: a rotation started now will\n   expire leaves before they re-issue. The window is recorded in the platform\n   limits reference and is not restated here.\n\n4. Publish the new root, then trigger re-issue in batches of ten services.\n\n5. Verify every counted service is serving a leaf signed by the new root before\n   retiring the old one.\n"
            }
          ],
